# ATG Beats Video Pipeline

Vertical 9:16 shorts. The host is silent through the teaching, holding a phone
toward camera whose screen demonstrates exactly what the voiceover is saying.
An ElevenLabs voiceover carries the lesson. Only the closing CTA is spoken
aloud on camera. The ATG Beats logo lands the ending.

The thing that makes these work is that **the visual explains the spoken line**
— not the mood of it. That's the whole method, and it's why the storyboard
comes before any animation prompt.

## What we're optimising for

Conversions, not views. The reference data: the best video by views (5K+)
produced zero clients; a 669-view video produced two real clients. When a
choice trades reach for clarity of offer, take clarity.

Three consequences that shape every video:

- **Hooks use micro-result + "without."** Name one small specific result and
  the outdated thing people already tried and failed at. "5 release videos
  from 1 song — without spending hours in an editor." This beats vague
  curiosity bait on conversions even when it pulls fewer raw views.
- **Open already in motion.** Drop-off data shows viewers leaving at 0:02,
  before the first voiceover line even lands. No static shot settling into
  frame, no fade-in. Something is moving, cutting or popping before 2s.
- **Rotate the CTA.** Don't run "Comment VIDEO" every time. Resource-unlock
  style CTAs are worth testing too.

For artist-facing lessons, teach two things together: the technical mechanics
(clarity, context, curiosity) *and* that the song itself has to solve a problem
or hit an emotion. No visual system fixes a song that doesn't connect.

## Production order

Follow this order. Each step depends on the one before it, and skipping to
animation prompts is the specific failure this pipeline exists to prevent.

1. Write the voiceover script as one continuous read.
2. Break it into visual beats — `references/storyboard.md`.
3. Match each phrase to a specific visual action.
4. Organise the beats into four rows of three.
5. Write one detailed 12-panel storyboard prompt.
6. Generate the storyboard image — **and show Makari for approval before
   animating anything.** This gate is not optional; he asked for it directly.
7. Generate the voiceover as a single ElevenLabs generation, then measure and
   split it — `references/voiceover.md`.
8. Write one animation prompt per row — `references/animation.md`.
9. Animate each row as a separate full-screen clip, sized to its span.
10. Assemble — `references/assembly.md`. Live speech only on the final CTA.

Asset naming and where files live: `references/assets.md`.

## Never name anyone in a prompt

No artist name, stage name, brand name or character name in any Flow prompt,
image or video. Describe the subject as `the person in the first attached
reference image` and let the attached reference carry identity. Flow may reject
a generation outright when a name is present, even with the right reference
attached.

The only exception is text that must literally appear on screen — put that in
quotation marks and say where it appears.

## Attach the character chip first

Every time, for images and video both: `@me` **first**, then the storyboard or
panel image. Attaching the style image first silently prevents the character
chip from binding and Flow generates a stranger. This reproduces reliably — it
is an ordering bug, not a model limitation.

Steps 6 and 7 can run in either order, but both must be done before any
animation prompt is written — the storyboard decides *what* happens and the
voiceover timings decide *how long* each row has to do it.

## The locked look

**Host.** Tag **`@me`** for the avatar. It's account-level so it works in any
project, unlike a character asset which is scoped to the project it was built
in.

**Wardrobe — paste this block verbatim into every prompt.** Not a paraphrase,
not a shortened version, and not "as established above." Sleeve length drifts
between clips in the same set when the description is loose — one batch came
back mixing short and long sleeves, which cannot be fixed in the edit.

> He wears a fitted solid black short-sleeve crew-neck t-shirt with the sleeves
> ending at mid-bicep, and two layered gold Cuban-link chains, a shorter one
> sitting at the collarbone and a longer one hanging below it. The shirt must
> stay short-sleeve for the entire clip, never long-sleeve, never a jacket,
> never a hoodie, and both chains must stay identical throughout with no change
> in length or count.

Change shirt colour by editing the colour word in that block, never by editing
a saved asset. Keep the gold chains regardless — they're part of the brand read.
Anything else that must match across a set gets the same treatment: spelled out
in full, in every prompt.

**Studio.** Dark premium home music-production studio, studio monitors and MIDI
keyboard, acoustic panels, **warm gold accent lighting and soft haze**,
cinematic realistic lighting, shallow depth of field.

**No blue or cyan anywhere.** An earlier version of this look used electric
blue and cyan LED accents with purple haze; it was rejected. Say "no blue or
cyan tones anywhere" in the prompt explicitly — the model reaches for cyan on
its own for anything tech-flavoured.

**Graphics live inside the phone.** A real smartphone held toward camera with
realistic app-style screens — rounded interface panels, natural shadows, clean
typography, realistic reflections, generic platform icons rather than
copyrighted logos. This replaced an earlier floating-hologram look that tested
worse. No holograms, no fantasy screens in the air, no cartoon UI, no random
glowing icons.

**Movement.** Camera punch-ins, phone close-ups, over-the-shoulder angles.

## Voiceover — one continuous read

elevenlabs.io → Text to Speech. Voice **Atg Mkari**, model **Eleven
Multilingual v2**. Roughly **17.6 characters per second** for estimating length.

**Generate the whole script as a single generation. Do not chunk it.** Chunked
audio butted together has audible seams and slightly wrong rhythm at every
join; one continuous read keeps the breath and pacing natural the whole way
through. Leave the CTA line out — that comes from the Flow clip itself, so you
see and hear him say it.

Because the audio is continuous, row boundaries come from *inside* it rather
than from file lengths. Find them by measuring, never by estimating from
character counts:

```bash
python3 scripts/assemble.py --timings VO_full.mp3          # needs faster-whisper
ffmpeg -i VO_full.mp3 -af "silencedetect=noise=-30dB:d=0.15" -f null -   # always works
```

Cut at the midpoint of each detected pause and verify every segment starts in
true silence before building. Full procedure in `references/voiceover.md`.

Those timestamps become each row's `end` in the build config, and the row clip
is trimmed to its own span.

This is also the moment the storyboard timecodes become real — the beats were
drafted against the script, and now they get pinned to the actual audio.

## Audio — locked

- **Instrumental** at constant level, **no sidechain ducking**, `volume=0.1237`
  (−18.2 dB). Trim to the video's full length and let it play all the way
  through, including under the logo.
- Voiceover at full level over the top.
- **Mute everything else** — every teaching clip, the CTA clip's background,
  and the logo's own bass hit. Flow generates speech audio even on silent
  clips and it will fight the voiceover.
- Limiter on the master at 0.95.
- Targets: speech ≈ −17 dB mean, music-only tail ≈ −32 dB mean, peaks under −2.

**Default new beds to up-tempo trap**, ~140 BPM, generated in Suno with
Instrumental on. Suno returns two versions — pick one and move on rather than
asking which. The gospel-trap recipe is the alternate, for explicitly gospel
content. Both prompts are in `references/assembly.md`.

## Logo outro

`ATG_Logo_Outro.mp4` — trim to **5 seconds**, scale to **105%**, audio muted,
instrumental continues underneath. No captions over it.

## Captions

Burned in. Poppins Bold 78px, pure white, 6px black outline, no shadow, bottom
centred 18% up from the frame bottom. Three-word groups with the spoken word
at full opacity and its neighbours dimmed, small scale pop on each new group.

Word timings come from `faster-whisper` on the voiceover, not from guessing:

```bash
pip install --break-system-packages faster-whisper
```

Force the comment keyword to uppercase afterwards — Whisper lowercases it.

## Assembly

`scripts/assemble.py` takes a JSON config and does the whole build: trims each
row clip to its voiceover chunk, mixes at constant level, transcribes for word
timings, builds the caption file and burns it in. Read the script header for
the config shape. `references/assembly.md` has the raw ffmpeg if you need to
debug.

Use ffmpeg, not CapCut — CapCut froze in production when a background Siri
window took modal focus over the import dialog, and ffmpeg gives frame-exact
trims a timeline drag can't.

## Verify before delivering

- Something is moving before the 2-second mark
- Mouth closed on every teaching clip
- Same man, same wardrobe, same studio, same phone in every clip
- Phone screen text is readable and says what it's supposed to
- No storyboard grid, panel borders or white gaps visible in any clip
- Hands look right — no extra or warped fingers
- Cuts land on sentence boundaries
- CTA delivers its line once (Flow repeats lines to pad — see
  `references/flow.md`)
- No captions over the logo
- Export 720x1280 or 1080x1920, 24fps, H.264, `-movflags +faststart`

Pull frames at several timestamps and actually look at them. Nearly every
failure in this pipeline is visual and silent — a drifted face, an unreadable
screen, a visible panel border. Nothing in any log will tell you.
